(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-external-middle-ear-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"External and Middle Ear Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which description fits normal cerumen?","choices":["A protective, thick, oily secretion","A watery, clear secretion","A purulent discharge","A dry keratin flake from the drum","A blood-stained fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is protective rather than merely waste. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which statement do patients with otosclerosis characteristically make?","choices":["Hearing is worse with background noise","The ear feels full after a cold","The room spins on rolling over","The ear itches constantly","Hearing is better with background noise"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Forced exhalation against resistance carries which specific caution?","choices":["Avoid it during active nasal discharge","Avoid it in anyone over sixty-five","Avoid it if the patient wears hearing aids","Avoid it in the morning","Avoid it after eating"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It can push infected secretions into the middle ear and cause acute otitis media. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What should be done with tissue avulsed from the auricle?","choices":["Discard it as non-viable","Freeze it for later grafting","Submit it for pathology only","Leave it at the scene","Bring it for reattachment"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — reattachment is often successful. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which class of antibiotic is required for necrotizing external otitis?","choices":["Narrow-spectrum penicillin","Topical steroid alone","Antipseudomonal","Antifungal","Antiviral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ciprofloxacin is the named example. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which measure is recommended for a frequent flyer with repeated ear barotrauma?","choices":["Tympanostomy tubes","Permanent avoidance of all flying","Daily oral antibiotics","Routine myringotomy before every flight","A hearing aid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — considered when episodes recur despite ordinary measures. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"At what age does acute otitis media peak?","choices":["Around two years","Around six months","Around six years","Around twelve years","Around forty years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the commonest paediatric ear disease at that age. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which everyday item is named as a risk factor for otitis externa through canal occlusion?","choices":["A bicycle helmet","A pillow","A face mask","Hearing aids","Reading glasses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, along with headphones. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which examination findings accompany carcinoma of the ear canal?","choices":["A retracted but intact drum","Bilateral symmetrical bony growths","White curd-like material in the canal","A normal canal and drum","A friable canal with bloody discharge"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, with an abnormal growth visible. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the consequence of failing to drain an auricular haematoma early?","choices":["Progression to cholesteatoma","Cauliflower ear","Permanent sensorineural hearing loss","Chronic vertigo","Facial paralysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — permanent cartilage deformity. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Why must irrigation be avoided when an organic foreign body is in the canal?","choices":["Water causes chemical burns","Organic material swells when wet","Water dissolves organic material","Irrigation always perforates the drum","Organic material becomes sharp when wet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which lodges it harder. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Within what period should an auricular haematoma be drained?","choices":["Within six weeks","Drainage is never required","Within seven days","Within one hour","Within thirty days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After that, granulation tissue makes drainage much harder. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which examination finding indicates an auricular haematoma?","choices":["Tenderness on moving the tragus","Purulent canal discharge","A retracted drum","Loss of the cartilaginous landmarks","A perforated tympanic membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with swelling and bruising of the auricle. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which symptom should raise suspicion of cholesteatoma?","choices":["Discharge with a tender, swollen canal","Sudden vertigo with vomiting","Itching relieved by drops","Pain that stops abruptly","Recurrent discharge without external canal infection"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — that combination is the giveaway. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In which part of the external canal are the glands that produce cerumen found?","choices":["The inner third","Throughout the whole canal","The middle ear","The mastoid air cells","The outer third"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The cartilaginous outer third carries the sebaceous and ceruminous glands. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Alongside prompt repair, what is the other critical priority in auricular laceration?","choices":["Infection prevention","Immediate audiometry","Computed tomography of the head","Systemic corticosteroids","Long-term splinting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the two are named together. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which history point most changes the organisms to worry about in a child with acute otitis media?","choices":["Birth weight","Number of siblings","Season of the year","Whether the child was breastfed","Immunisation status"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. An unimmunised child puts Haemophilus influenzae back on the list. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the treatment of fungal external ear infection?","choices":["Observation only","Debris removal and topical antifungals","Oral antifungals alone","Antipseudomonal antibiotics","Surgical canal reconstruction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cleaning matters as much as the drops. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which triad of symptoms characterises acute otitis media?","choices":["Itching, discharge and canal tenderness","Facial weakness, severe pain and discharge","Painless discharge and a chalky mass","Otalgia, fever and hearing loss","Vertigo, tinnitus and hearing loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is mastoiditis?","choices":["Bony overgrowth fixing the stapes","A vascular tumour of the middle ear","Infection spreading into the mastoid air cells","Infection of the external canal skin","A keratin collection in the middle ear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, as a complication of acute otitis media. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which ossicle does otosclerosis affect?","choices":["The cochlea","The tympanic membrane","The stapes","The malleus","The incus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bony overgrowth eventually fixes it. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which three factors decide whether otitis media with effusion is treated in a child?","choices":["Ear canal size, wax volume, and drum colour","Duration, degree of hearing loss, and effect on speech and language","Age, sex, and family history","Fever, pain score, and white cell count","Season, school attendance, and travel plans"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those three drive the decision, not the appearance of the drum. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What distinguishes otitis media with effusion from acute otitis media?","choices":["The tympanic membrane is always perforated","It only occurs in adults","It always follows trauma","There is fluid but no acute infection","There is infection but no fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which imaging study is used to assess the extent of a severe cholesteatoma?","choices":["Ultrasound","Positron emission tomography","Computed tomography","Magnetic resonance imaging","Plain radiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is especially useful when the drum is opaque. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What causes a keloid of the ear?","choices":["Chronic infection","Sun exposure","Loud noise","Trauma, classically ear piercing","Cold water exposure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the characteristic otoscopic finding in eustachian tube dysfunction?","choices":["A retracted tympanic membrane","A bulging tympanic membrane","A perforated tympanic membrane","A chalky white mass behind the drum","Blood behind the tympanic membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Negative middle ear pressure pulls the drum inward. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is done before instilling drops for otitis externa?","choices":["Give oral antibiotics for a day","Irrigate with hot water","Apply a pressure dressing","Remove debris from the canal","Take an audiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — drops cannot reach inflamed skin through debris. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What establishes the diagnosis of carcinoma of the ear canal?","choices":["Audiometry","Tympanometry","Biopsy","Computed tomography","Culture of the discharge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which subtype of chronic otitis media has a dry perforation with no active infection?","choices":["Adhesive","Benign","Chronic suppurative","With effusion","Necrotizing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is named as the treatment for bacterial acute otitis media?","choices":["Nitrofurantoin","Amoxicillin","Ciprofloxacin","Vancomycin","Metronidazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 18"}]};
